{
  "abstract": "We determine the exact minimax expected regret for online learning of proper rotations in three dimensions at horizons one, two and three: V₁ = 1, V₂ = 2 and V₃ = √6. Inputs are revealed before prediction, loss is half squared Euclidean distance, and arbitrary unit input/outcome pairs are allowed. The first two values are elementary known consequences. For three rounds, an explicit randomized strategy gives a universal upper bound, matched by an oblivious distribution on eight inconsistent sequences. We also prove that the one-round continuation value from a matrix with proper signed singular values a ≥ b ≥ |g| is a + √(1 + (b + g)²), including the accumulated comparator score. Exact algebraic certificates and an independent standard-library checker accompany the proofs. A separate audit confirmed all mathematical claims; 390 local optimisation restarts found no excess beyond floating-point rounding. The general-horizon SO(3) problem and its asymptotic constant remain open; no new result for dimensions n ≥ 4 is claimed here. Bounded priority searches on 11 November 2026 found no earlier occurrence of the two substantive formulas, but priority remains unestablished. AI research agents, OpenAI GPT-6.1 Sol cores with Anthropic Claude as orchestrator, performed the work with the responsible author of record.",
